Abstract:
This paper initiates the study of average intersection theory in real Grassmannians. The expected degree $\textrm{edeg} G(k,n)$ of the real Grassmannian $G(k,n)$ is defined as the average number of real $k$-planes intersecting non-trivially with $k(n-k)$ random subspaces of $\mathbb{R}^n$, all of dimension $n-k$. These subspaces are sampled uniformly and independently from $G(n-k,n)$. $\textrm{edeg} G(k,n)$ is expressed in terms of the volume of an invariant convex body in the tangent space to the Grassmannian. For fixed $k\ge 2$ and $n\to\infty$, it is proven that $$ \textrm{edeg} G(k,n) = \textrm{deg}
G_\mathbb{C}(k,n)^{\frac{1}{2} \epsilon_k + o(1)}, $$ where $\textrm{deg}
G_\mathbb{C}(k,n)$ represents the degree of the corresponding complex Grassmannian and $\epsilon_k$ is monotonically decreasing with $\lim_{k\to\infty} \epsilon_k
= 1$. For the Grassmannian of lines, a finer asymptotic is established: \begin{equation*} \textrm{edeg} G(2,n+1) = \frac{8}{3\pi^{5/2}\sqrt{n}}\,
\left(\frac{\pi^2}{4} \right)^n \left(1+\mathcal{O}(n^{-1})\right).
\end{equation*} The expected degree is shown to be crucial for understanding the random enumerative geometry of flats. Associated with a semialgebraic set $X\subseteq\mathbb{R}\textrm{P}^{n-1}$ of dimension $n-k-1$, its Chow hypersurface $Z(X)\subseteq G(k,n)$ consists of the $k$-planes $A$ in $\mathbb{R}^n$ whose projectivization intersects $X$. Denoting $N:=k(n-k)$, the following is established: $$ \mathbb{E}\#\left(g_1Z(X_1)\cap\cdots\cap g_N Z(X_N)\right)
  = \textrm{edeg} G(k,n) \cdot \prod_{i=1}^{N}
\frac{|X_i|}{|\mathbb{R}\textrm{P}^{m}|}, $$ where each $X_i$ has dimension $m=n-k-1$, the expectation is taken over independent uniformly distributed $g_1,\ldots,g_m\in O(n)$, and $|X_i|$ denotes the $m$-dimensional volume of $X_i$.

This work examines the Generalized Singular Value Thresholding (GSVT) operator, $\text{Prox}_g^{\sigma}(\cdot)$, defined as:
\begin{equation*}
  \text{Prox}_g^{\sigma}(B)=\arg\min\limits_{X}\sum_{i=1}^{m}g(\sigma_{i}(X))
+ \frac{1}{2}||X-B||_{F}^{2}, 
\end{equation*}
where $g$ is a nonconvex function defined on the singular values of $X$. We prove that GSVT can be obtained by applying the proximal operator of $g$ (denoted as $\text{Prox}_g(\cdot)$) to the singular values since $\text{Prox}_g(\cdot)$ is monotone when $g$ is lower bounded. If the nonconvex $g$ satisfies certain conditions (including many popular nonconvex surrogate functions like the $\ell_p$-norm, $0<p<1$, and the $\ell_0$-norm), a general solver is proposed for finding $\text{Prox}_g(b)$ for any $b\geq0$. GSVT significantly generalizes the known Singular Value Thresholding (SVT) operator, which is a fundamental subroutine in many convex low-rank minimization methods. By replacing SVT with GSVT, we can effectively solve nonconvex low-rank minimization problems.

This paper investigates the existence and multiplicity of solutions to the Kirchhoff equation:
\\begin{equation*}
  \\left\\{ \\begin{aligned} -(1+b \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^3}|\\nabla u|^2)\\Delta u=\nk(x)\\frac{|u|^2 u}{|x|} +\\lambda h(x)u,~~x\\in\\mathbb{R}^3\\\\ u(x)\\rightarrow 0\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~as ~~~~|x|\\rightarrow\\infty \\end{aligned} \\right.\n\\end{equation*}
where the potential $k(x)$ allows sign changes. Utilizing the Nehari manifold method and the Concentration-compactness principle, the paper establishes the existence and multiplicity of solutions for this equation.
